A batch of security-relevant bugs found during PR review of #94 (email verification) — each
too small to justify its own file, so they're tracked together here. Unlike the grouped-file
convention for a single staged initiative, these entries aren't ordered or dependent on each
other; they're just being scheduled and fixed together on the same branch.

---

## email_verified claim comparison never matches, so Google auto-confirm silently never fires

### Status
Fixed.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
`ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`'s `OnPostConfirmationAsync` compares the `email_verified` claim
against the lowercase literal `"true"`, but ASP.NET Core's `JsonKeyClaimAction` (used by
`Program.cs`'s `MapJsonKey("email_verified", ..., ClaimValueTypes.Boolean)`) stores a JSON
boolean claim value via `JsonElement.ToString()`, which returns the capitalized
`bool.TrueString`/`bool.FalseString` (`"True"`/`"False"`) for `JsonValueKind.True`/`False` — not
lowercase `"true"`/`"false"`.

### Reported Behavior
Not user-reported — found during review of #94, after the fix in
`BUGS-external-login-email-verification-claim.ospec` was implemented. The comparison
`info.Principal.FindFirst("email_verified")?.Value == "true"` can never be true for a claim
populated through `MapJsonKey`, so `emailIsProviderVerified` is always `false`.

### Expected Behavior
A first-time Google sign-in where Google's `email_verified` claim is `true` gets
`EmailConfirmed = true` and is signed in directly, matching the acceptance criteria of
`BUGS-external-login-email-verification-claim.ospec`.

### Root Cause
`JsonKeyClaimAction.Run` (in `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.OAuth`) converts the mapped
`JsonElement` to a claim string via `.ToString()`. For a JSON boolean, `JsonElement.ToString()`
returns `.NET`'s `bool.TrueString`/`FalseString` (`"True"`/`"False"`), not the lowercase JSON
literal spelling. The existing check in `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs` was written and tested against
a hand-constructed claim (`new Claim("email_verified", "true")` in
`ExternalLoginConfirmationTests.cs`), which never exercises the real `MapJsonKey` conversion
path, so the mismatch went uncaught.

### Proposed Fix
Change the comparison in `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`'s `OnPostConfirmationAsync` to compare
case-insensitively, e.g.
`string.Equals(info.Principal.FindFirst("email_verified")?.Value, bool.TrueString, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)`
or `StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase` against `"true"`. Also update
`ExternalLoginConfirmationTests.cs`'s claim construction to use the capitalized form
(`bool.TrueString`/`bool.FalseString`) so the test actually reflects what `MapJsonKey` produces,
rather than a value that happens to never occur in production.

### Acceptance Criteria
- A first-time Google sign-in whose `Input.Email` matches the provider's `Email` claim, and whose
  `email_verified` claim is the real (capitalized) `MapJsonKey`-produced `"True"`, gets
  `EmailConfirmed = true` and is signed in directly.
- `ExternalLoginConfirmationTests.cs` constructs its `email_verified` claim value the same way
  `JsonKeyClaimAction` actually produces it, not a hand-typed lowercase string.

### Out of Scope
- Any other external provider — same scope note as the original claim-checking fix.

