## RegisterConfirmation page is an unauthenticated account-existence oracle

### Status
Fixed.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
`RegisterConfirmationModel.OnGetAsync` looks up a user by an unauthenticated `email`
query-string parameter and returns `NotFound()` when no account matches, `Page()` (200) when one
does — letting anyone probe the endpoint to learn whether a given email address has a
registered account.

### Reported Behavior
Not user-reported — found during review of #94. `GET /Identity/Account/RegisterConfirmation?email=<address>`
returns a 404 for an unregistered address and a 200 for a registered one, with no rate limiting
or authentication.

### Expected Behavior
The page's response does not disclose whether the given email address has a registered account,
consistent with the anti-enumeration behavior this same feature already provides elsewhere
(`ApplicationSignInManager`'s password-before-status ordering, `ResendEmailConfirmation.cshtml.cs`
showing an identical message regardless of account existence).

### Root Cause
`OnGetAsync` was written to look up the user purely to render a nicer message (e.g. confirming
which address the confirmation email was sent to), without considering that the presence/absence
of that lookup result is itself observable through the response's HTTP status code.

### Proposed Fix
Make `OnGetAsync` return the same `Page()` result (200) regardless of whether
`FindByEmailAsync(email)` finds a user, the same way `ResendEmailConfirmation.cshtml.cs` already
does. If the page needs to display the submitted email back to the user, echo the query-string
value directly rather than a value sourced from the lookup, so no branch of the response depends
on whether the account exists.

### Acceptance Criteria
- `GET /Identity/Account/RegisterConfirmation?email=<address>` returns the same status code
  (200) whether or not `<address>` has a registered account.
- The page's rendered content does not otherwise reveal whether the account exists.
- A new test covers both the existing-account and non-existing-account cases and asserts they
  produce indistinguishable responses.

### Out of Scope
- Rate limiting the endpoint — the response-shape fix removes the signal being probed for; rate
  limiting is a separate, broader concern not scoped to this page.

---

## ApplicationSignInManager resets lockout before 2FA is verified

### Status
Fixed.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
`ApplicationSignInManager.CheckPasswordSignInAsync`'s success path unconditionally calls
`ResetLockout(user)`, but the base `SignInManager<TUser>.CheckPasswordSignInCoreAsync` only
resets the lockout counter when two-factor authentication is disabled or already remembered on
the current device, deferring the reset until the second factor is verified.

### Reported Behavior
Not user-reported — found during review of #94. For an account with 2FA enabled and not
remembered on the signing-in device, a correct password immediately resets the failed-access
counter via this override, before any second-factor challenge is issued or verified.

### Expected Behavior
The failed-access-attempt counter is only reset once sign-in is actually complete — i.e. either
2FA is disabled for the account, or the 2FA client is already remembered — matching the base
`SignInManager`'s own ordering, which this class's doc comments claim to preserve.

### Root Cause
The override calls `ResetLockout(user)` directly after a successful password check, without
reproducing the base implementation's guard
(`alwaysLockout || !await IsTwoFactorEnabledAsync(user) || await IsTwoFactorClientRememberedAsync(user)`)
that determines whether resetting at this point is actually correct.

### Proposed Fix
Reproduce the base `SignInManager`'s guard before calling `ResetLockout(user)` in
`ApplicationSignInManager.CheckPasswordSignInAsync`, so the reset is deferred exactly as it is in
the framework for a 2FA-enabled, not-yet-remembered account.

### Acceptance Criteria
- A correct password against a 2FA-enabled account that isn't remembered on the current device
  does not reset the failed-access counter until the second factor is verified.
- A correct password against an account with 2FA disabled (or already remembered) still resets
  the counter immediately, as today.
- A new test in `ApplicationSignInManagerTests.cs` covers a 2FA-enabled, not-remembered account
  with a correct password and asserts the lockout counter is left untouched.

### Out of Scope
- Any change to the wrong-password/lockout-triggering path — only the success-path reset timing
  is in scope here.
